      </a>
    </section>

    <section style="text-align:center; padding:2rem 1rem 2rem;">
      <h1 style="font-size:2.25rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:0.5rem; line-height:1.2;">
        What Is Western Black and White Photography?
      </h1>
      <p style="font-size:0.95rem; color:#888; margin-top:0.75rem; letter-spacing:0.02em;">
        Not a filter. Not a style. A structural decision that changes what the image has to earn.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="early-examples-section">
      <div class="early-examples-shell">
        <p class="early-examples-kicker">See the Monochrome Standard</p>
        <h2 class="early-examples-title">Western black and white photography begins when tone has to carry the whole image.</h2>
        <p class="early-examples-intro">
          Cowboy portraits, Western narratives, and Native American portrait work all change when color is removed. Shadow, posture, expression, and restraint become the image's structure.
        </p>

        <div class="early-examples-grid early-examples-grid--three">
          <figure>
            <a href={`${COWBOY_BW_PATH}/${image1.id}`}>
              <img src={image1.src} alt={image1.alt} loading="eager" />
            </a>
            <figcaption>Posture and character without color warmth.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure>
            <a href={`${NARRATIVE_BW_PATH}/${image2.id}`}>
              <img src={image2.src} alt={image2.alt} loading="lazy" />
            </a>
            <figcaption>Shadow and implication doing the work of color.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure>
            <a href={`${NATIVE_BW_PATH}/${image3.id}`}>
              <img src={image3.src} alt={image3.alt} loading="lazy" />
            </a>
